WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.740
Picture this. It is Jerusalem, the hour just before dawn. The air is thick with dust.

00:07.100 --> 00:12.740
You can almost smell the crushed olives. And there is just this sheer unfiltered tension

00:12.740 --> 00:18.640
everywhere. The absolute darkest part of the night. Exactly. And we are moving through the dark

00:18.640 --> 00:25.620
with a squad of Roman soldiers. And they are hunting one specific man, a fugitive teacher.

00:25.780 --> 00:28.860
Yeah, they have the perimeter secured. Right. They have the element of surprise.

00:28.860 --> 00:35.640
And finally, in the shadows of this olive grove, they corner him. The soldiers lunge. The captain's

00:35.640 --> 00:40.220
hands close tightly around the rough woven fabric of the man's cloak. He thinks he has him. He

00:40.220 --> 00:45.980
actually shouts that he has him. But in the very next fraction of a second, the man simply slips out

00:45.980 --> 00:50.400
of the garment. Just leaves it behind. Completely. He disappears into the darkness of the trees,

00:50.800 --> 00:55.560
totally naked, leaving only this empty coarse fabric hanging lifelessly in the hands of the Roman guard.

00:55.560 --> 01:01.960
It's such a phenomenal opening sequence. You have the torchlight flickering over this empty piece of

01:01.960 --> 01:06.900
cloth. The soldiers are just standing there in total disbelief. Because, you know, they operated

01:06.900 --> 01:09.940
under the assumption that if you capture the physical material, you capture the man.

01:10.060 --> 01:15.880
Okay, let's unpack this. Because this single moment, this escape, is the bedrock of the source

01:15.880 --> 01:18.880
material we are diving into today. It really sets the stage.

01:18.880 --> 01:26.040
It does. We're looking at this incredible graphic parable by Fliction titled The Cloak. A parable of

01:26.040 --> 01:31.120
identity, pursuit, and the geometry of escape. Also known as The Fugitive Teacher, Volume 1.

01:31.260 --> 01:36.740
Yeah, exactly. And the book sets up its entire philosophical premise right here in the dirt with

01:36.740 --> 01:41.900
this discarded cloak. The Romans look at it and think they've succeeded in pinning down their target's

01:41.900 --> 01:46.620
location. But the parable introduces a concept that completely shatters that illusion.

01:46.620 --> 01:53.020
Yes. The text says, and I love this quote, they captured the state, the process escaped.

01:53.180 --> 01:58.200
That distinction right there. State versus process. That is the exact lens we need to

01:58.200 --> 01:59.940
analyze this entire story through.

02:00.240 --> 02:05.460
And that is our mission for this deep dive. We are going to explore the massive gap between those

02:05.460 --> 02:11.280
two ideas. Because this goes so far beyond just some historical thriller set in ancient Judea.

02:11.300 --> 02:14.660
Oh, absolutely. It's a fundamental examination of identity itself.

02:14.660 --> 02:20.360
Right. We want to show you how we, all of us listening right now, how we constantly make the

02:20.360 --> 02:25.500
exact same error the Romans made. We define ourselves by where we are, our current state,

02:25.860 --> 02:28.260
rather than the trajectory of where we can go.

02:28.600 --> 02:33.360
Because if we connect this to the bigger picture, you know, looking at bureaucratic structures,

02:33.560 --> 02:36.320
rigid corporate systems, or even our own personal psychology,

02:36.320 --> 02:41.940
failure almost always stems from confusing the snapshot with the ongoing reality. We think a

02:41.940 --> 02:46.020
person is their current job title, or a system is just its current output.

02:46.120 --> 02:50.080
But the parable is arguing that identity belongs to the path, not the snapshot.

02:50.280 --> 02:55.240
Exactly. A state is just a snapshot. It is an observation frozen at one specific instant in time.

02:55.320 --> 02:56.700
Like a single frame of a movie?

02:57.060 --> 03:02.360
Right. The Romans arrested a moment in time, but the reality of the fugitive was already moving into

03:02.360 --> 03:08.100
the next second. He was creating a new set of possibilities. He left his state behind so he

03:08.100 --> 03:12.400
could preserve his future. Which brings us to the immediate aftermath of that escape.

03:12.520 --> 03:15.620
Yeah. Because the visual storytelling here is just incredible.

03:15.660 --> 03:20.720
It really shifts gears. It does. So it's dawn. The Roman commander is standing in the streets

03:20.720 --> 03:26.040
looking at a wax tablet with the fugitive's physical description. He is preparing to lock down

03:26.040 --> 03:32.780
the entire city. He's got the state. He's got the data. Exactly. But by mid-morning, this entirely new,

03:33.180 --> 03:37.940
completely unpredicted problem has materialized. He looks out over the markets and the squares,

03:38.260 --> 03:44.920
and suddenly, literally everyone in the city is wearing the exact same undyed, white, rough-woven cloak.

03:44.920 --> 03:49.400
It's brilliant. It functions as the spontaneous, decentralized camouflage.

03:49.980 --> 03:54.380
Right. By dawn, the local network knew an escape had occurred. And by mid-morning,

03:54.380 --> 03:58.880
the community's response was actively disrupting the empire's algorithm for identification.

03:59.500 --> 04:03.180
Because the Roman methodology relies entirely on comparing visual data

04:03.180 --> 04:07.320
against a static record. They check a face, they check a garment, they verify the data,

04:07.380 --> 04:11.640
and they move on. Yeah. They believe that if they just process enough variables,

04:11.780 --> 04:15.520
if they brute force the data matching, they will isolate the correct man.

04:15.800 --> 04:18.820
But if the Romans are checking every face and every cloak,

04:19.340 --> 04:23.340
why is the text saying they are looking at the wrong thing entirely?

04:23.340 --> 04:27.080
Well, because the fugitive purposefully discarded his state,

04:27.580 --> 04:32.520
the book explicitly compares that abandoned cloak in the dirt to a shed skin.

04:32.740 --> 04:37.120
Oh, wow. A shed skin. I want to look closer at that biological reality for a second.

04:37.440 --> 04:40.940
Because shedding his skin isn't just dropping a piece of clothing like taking off a jacket.

04:41.060 --> 04:41.760
No, not at all.

04:41.880 --> 04:46.240
For a reptile, it requires a massive amount of energy. It's a period of extreme vulnerability.

04:46.240 --> 04:50.560
And what is left behind is this perfect, highly detailed,

04:50.760 --> 04:53.940
but completely dead replica of what the creature used to be.

04:54.460 --> 04:57.240
And that dead replica is precisely what the Romans are interrogating.

04:58.060 --> 04:58.240
Yes.

04:58.440 --> 05:02.840
A shed skin doesn't offer a single piece of predictive data about where the snake is going next,

05:02.980 --> 05:05.700
or even what size it currently is.

05:05.880 --> 05:07.860
It only proves where it used to be.

05:08.120 --> 05:09.000
It is backward looking.

05:09.000 --> 05:15.840
Entirely. The Roman error in categorization assumes that preserving the state is synonymous with capturing the trajectory.

05:16.960 --> 05:22.160
Mathematically speaking, they are confusing the historical record of a variable with its derivative.

05:22.560 --> 05:23.820
Okay, that makes total sense.

05:24.140 --> 05:27.400
And I think we need to look at how this methodology manifests today.

05:27.540 --> 05:30.000
Because the Roman obsession with state,

05:30.720 --> 05:36.180
it feels remarkably similar to how algorithmic feeds define us on social media.

05:36.420 --> 05:38.420
Oh, that is a highly accurate parallel.

05:38.420 --> 05:39.720
Right. Think about it.

05:39.920 --> 05:43.560
An algorithm looks at the articles you clicked yesterday, the videos you watched last week.

05:43.640 --> 05:45.380
The things you hovered over for two seconds.

05:45.420 --> 05:50.860
Yeah. And it builds this highly detailed, perfectly categorized profile of your past actions.

05:51.140 --> 05:56.620
It captures your shed skin, and then it relentlessly feeds you content based on that dead replica,

05:57.140 --> 05:59.220
trapping you in a perpetual loop of who you were.

05:59.460 --> 06:02.420
Because the algorithm possesses the data of the moment,

06:03.000 --> 06:06.580
it assumes your identity is fixed to those historical coordinates.

06:06.680 --> 06:07.480
But we aren't fixed.

06:07.660 --> 06:08.140
Exactly.

06:08.420 --> 06:10.760
Human consciousness is a continuing process.

06:10.960 --> 06:13.520
It shifts, it learns, it gets bored, it seeks novelty.

06:14.260 --> 06:18.360
When an algorithm forces you into a feedback loop of your past state,

06:18.880 --> 06:20.860
it actively suppresses your trajectory.

06:21.460 --> 06:25.740
It limits your exposure to new ideas that would actually foster growth.

06:26.020 --> 06:26.240
Right.

06:26.360 --> 06:29.160
So the Romans are essentially acting like a broken algorithm.

06:29.640 --> 06:33.500
They are checking every face in a city where everyone now looks the same.

06:33.800 --> 06:35.400
They aren't just looking in the wrong place.

06:35.400 --> 06:37.420
They are looking at the wrong dimension of reality.

06:37.420 --> 06:41.360
They are equating material evidence with absolute truth.

06:41.360 --> 06:46.820
And later in the parable, affliction depicts a formal Roman trial to illustrate this exact point.

06:46.880 --> 06:47.620
Oh, I love this part.

06:47.620 --> 06:49.400
The tribunal has perfect evidence.

06:49.400 --> 06:51.100
The arrest records are flawless.

06:51.100 --> 06:53.240
The legal parameters are all met.

06:53.240 --> 07:00.360
The text points out that the bureaucratic process demands evidence and the evidence perfectly maps where the individual has been.

07:00.360 --> 07:00.640
Right.

07:00.700 --> 07:01.560
They have all the paperwork.

07:01.920 --> 07:04.980
But then the narrative delivers this incredibly sharp critique.

07:05.340 --> 07:05.780
It says,

07:06.460 --> 07:07.540
The case was closed.

07:07.920 --> 07:09.040
The journey continued.

07:09.460 --> 07:10.620
The paperwork is finished.

07:11.420 --> 07:13.700
But the reality is already miles away.

07:14.120 --> 07:18.280
The Romans are satisfied because they successfully validated a coordinate in the past.

07:18.280 --> 07:23.220
But human identity isn't bound to the physical material of a closed case file.

07:23.700 --> 07:27.820
Identity is bound to the landscape of what the source calls admissible futures.

07:28.200 --> 07:29.120
Admissible futures.

07:29.300 --> 07:31.360
We are definitely going to dig into that term.

07:31.640 --> 07:34.640
But first, we want to bring this directly to you, the listener, for a moment.

07:34.780 --> 07:36.620
It's important to reflect on this personally.

07:36.920 --> 07:37.620
It really is.

07:37.980 --> 07:40.220
Think about how often you might be doing this to yourself.

07:40.560 --> 07:43.980
Are you defining your own identity by a cloak you've already discarded?

07:43.980 --> 07:52.360
Maybe you are holding on to a past failure or a previous relationship or even a career peak you hit five years ago.

07:52.960 --> 07:55.780
A past success can be just as trapping as a past failure.

07:56.180 --> 07:56.540
Exactly.

07:56.680 --> 08:00.980
If you define yourself by a state you've already shed, you are acting as your own Roman guard.

08:01.700 --> 08:04.120
You are holding on to an empty piece of fabric in the dark,

08:04.280 --> 08:09.060
while the living, breathing process of who you actually are is just waiting to move forward.

08:09.060 --> 08:15.280
Because when we over-identify with a temporary state, we create incredible psychological brittleness.

08:15.720 --> 08:16.660
What do you mean by brittleness?

08:17.140 --> 08:21.180
Well, if your entire identity is anchored to a specific job title,

08:21.680 --> 08:24.960
the sudden loss of that job feels like total annihilation.

08:25.160 --> 08:27.860
Oh, right, because the thing you thought was you is gone.

08:27.980 --> 08:28.260
Yes.

08:28.700 --> 08:31.340
The substrate is gone, so you assume the pattern is dead.

08:31.340 --> 08:36.960
But if you identify with your trajectory, your adaptability, your capacity to learn, your underlying values,

08:37.600 --> 08:41.060
then losing the job is just a localized change.

08:41.260 --> 08:44.220
The process of who you are simply migrates and continues.

08:44.540 --> 08:44.980
Precisely.

08:45.160 --> 08:48.860
Which opens up a massive logistical problem for the Romans in our story.

08:49.380 --> 08:54.060
Because if looking for the physical cloak is a dead end, how do they actually track a moving process?

08:54.480 --> 08:55.500
They are totally stuck.

08:55.640 --> 08:55.860
Right.

08:56.140 --> 08:57.460
The city is locked down.

08:57.600 --> 08:58.740
The main gates are watched.

08:58.920 --> 09:00.840
Every known road is patrolled.

09:01.340 --> 09:07.200
And this leads us to this fascinating character, a Roman soldier named Gaius.

09:07.460 --> 09:10.440
Gaius is such a crucial figure in this parable.

09:10.500 --> 09:11.200
He really is.

09:11.720 --> 09:18.200
He's standing on an overlook, staring down at the geography of Judea, and he experiences a total paradigm shift.

09:18.360 --> 09:21.360
Gaius represents the analytical limit of the Roman worldview.

09:21.800 --> 09:24.580
He's reviewing the map of all known infrastructure.

09:24.780 --> 09:27.320
The paved roads, the garrisons, the checkpoints.

09:27.440 --> 09:28.660
The things the empire built.

09:28.660 --> 09:28.940
Right.

09:29.160 --> 09:35.760
And his commanding officers are frustrated because the scattered sightings of the fugitive make no logical sense based on the road network.

09:36.200 --> 09:37.800
They assume the data must be flawed.

09:37.800 --> 09:40.520
But Gaius realizes the flaw isn't in the sightings.

09:41.000 --> 09:44.800
The flaw is in the underlying geometry they're using to understand the world.

09:45.040 --> 09:45.640
Exactly.

09:45.860 --> 09:53.900
He looks down into a deep, jagged ravine miles from any paved surface and realizes the fugitive is moving through a completely different layer of reality.

09:54.380 --> 09:56.540
He says, not roads, water.

09:56.540 --> 10:00.140
This introduces the fundamental topological concept of the two maps.

10:00.300 --> 10:00.940
The two maps, yeah.

10:01.180 --> 10:05.940
Gaius realizes that two distinct geometries are occupying the exact same physical space.

10:06.680 --> 10:09.560
The first map is the visible engineered network.

10:09.980 --> 10:13.400
It's the roads the empire built to assert control and move armies.

10:13.640 --> 10:13.880
Right.

10:14.060 --> 10:18.300
In graph theory, we would formalize this visible network as a graph, G.

10:18.300 --> 10:23.860
It consists of vertices, which are the locations, and edges, which are the visible roads.

10:24.160 --> 10:26.080
So graph G is the official map of reality.

10:26.280 --> 10:28.360
It's the world as the empire demands it to be.

10:28.520 --> 10:31.840
But the fugitive is utilizing a completely hidden architecture.

10:32.200 --> 10:39.100
He's moving through dry wadis, ancient cistern systems, forgotten shepherd paths, and subterranean water channels.

10:39.160 --> 10:41.480
Stuff the Romans didn't build and barely even notice.

10:41.700 --> 10:42.220
Exactly.

10:42.720 --> 10:45.580
Fleshen designates this hidden network as G-star.

10:45.580 --> 10:50.580
It contains not just the engineered edges of the empire, but the organic hidden continuations.

10:51.120 --> 10:53.320
The paths shaped by the memory of water.

10:54.040 --> 10:59.420
I think the best way to conceptualize this is to think about modern city infrastructure versus local knowledge.

10:59.500 --> 11:00.820
Oh, that's a great way to put it.

11:01.040 --> 11:07.540
Like, if you open a standard GPS app during a massive city parade, or a severe traffic gridlock,

11:08.040 --> 11:13.720
the app which represents the official map, the G-network will tell you that movement is mathematically impossible.

11:13.720 --> 11:15.420
The main arteries are all red.

11:15.580 --> 11:15.740
Right.

11:15.840 --> 11:17.160
The screen is just glowing red.

11:17.240 --> 11:17.960
You are stuck.

11:18.540 --> 11:24.140
But a local who's lived in that neighborhood for 20 years doesn't use the main arteries.

11:24.360 --> 11:25.260
They know the workarounds.

11:25.360 --> 11:25.520
Yeah.

11:25.880 --> 11:31.960
They know the alleyways, the cut-throughs by the grocery store, that one pedestrian bridge that isn't clearly marked on any map.

11:32.320 --> 11:33.900
They are navigating G-star.

11:34.200 --> 11:37.620
Because they understand the hidden topology of the environment.

11:37.720 --> 11:37.900
Right.

11:37.900 --> 11:39.900
They aren't bound by the official constraints.

11:40.620 --> 11:44.720
And that local knowledge fundamentally changes the question being asked, doesn't it?

11:44.800 --> 11:45.280
It does.

11:45.720 --> 11:49.800
The Roman officers who are obsessed with state are asking, where is he right now?

11:50.320 --> 11:53.980
They want to isolate his exact spatial coordinates at a given time.

11:54.360 --> 11:59.540
But Gaius realizes that to track a master navigator, you have to ask a different question.

11:59.540 --> 12:01.560
You have to ask, where can he go?

12:02.120 --> 12:03.320
The concept of reachability.

12:03.740 --> 12:04.260
Precisely.

12:04.880 --> 12:10.660
In the source's notation, Gaius shifts from solving for a fixed location to calculating R of X.

12:10.820 --> 12:11.380
R X.

12:11.580 --> 12:15.280
Which is the set of all reachable futures from the fugitive's current position.

12:15.500 --> 12:18.720
This shift from location to potential is just revolutionary.

12:19.860 --> 12:21.960
Reachability isn't about where your feet are planted.

12:22.200 --> 12:24.260
It's about the volume of your remaining options.

12:24.380 --> 12:26.040
And options dictate power.

12:26.040 --> 12:26.560
Yes.

12:27.320 --> 12:29.240
Let's use an analogy of a chess game here.

12:29.380 --> 12:29.560
Okay.

12:29.800 --> 12:33.220
A piece's value isn't just the square it's sitting on right now.

12:33.440 --> 12:37.300
The real value is the number of moves it can legally make next.

12:37.980 --> 12:40.800
A knight in the center of the board has high reachability.

12:41.360 --> 12:46.140
A knight traps in the corner is weak, even if it's the exact same piece of carved wood.

12:46.240 --> 12:47.440
That is a perfect analogy.

12:47.760 --> 12:49.400
The fugitive isn't just running away.

12:49.560 --> 12:51.960
He is navigating through what remained possible.

12:51.960 --> 12:57.040
And if we look at how urban planning dictates economic mobility, for instance, a neighborhood

12:57.040 --> 12:59.780
might be physically located in the center of a wealthy city.

13:00.240 --> 13:04.340
But if it's cut off by a massive highway system with no pedestrian access and terrible

13:04.340 --> 13:05.060
public transit.

13:05.180 --> 13:08.000
Then their reachability is artificially reduced to near zero.

13:08.420 --> 13:08.740
Exactly.

13:09.100 --> 13:13.620
Their physical location is central, but their topological reality is completely isolated.

13:14.260 --> 13:18.340
The value of a position is defined entirely by its future possibilities.

13:18.340 --> 13:23.600
And the parable applies this to survival mechanics through what it calls the admissibility field.

13:23.960 --> 13:25.140
The admissibility field.

13:25.280 --> 13:27.620
The fugitive is not running blindly in a panic.

13:28.220 --> 13:31.820
He is making calculated decisions based on the landscape of reachability.

13:32.400 --> 13:36.620
He moves away from the visible nodes where the Romans have reduced the admissible futures

13:36.620 --> 13:39.560
to zero, like guard towers and checkpoints.

13:39.800 --> 13:43.380
And he flows toward the dry wadis where the pathways branch and multiply.

13:43.560 --> 13:44.060
Exactly.

13:44.060 --> 13:49.480
Gaius actually writes a field note in his journal about this that is absolutely staggering.

13:49.740 --> 13:50.840
Oh, I have that quote right here.

13:51.260 --> 13:51.580
He writes,

13:52.020 --> 13:55.760
A search of G may return nothing, even when a path exists in G star.

13:56.280 --> 14:00.320
To search only the visible graph is to accept blindness as methodology.

14:00.900 --> 14:03.500
It's a profound indictment of systemic hubris.

14:04.220 --> 14:06.380
The empire engineered the visible roads.

14:06.520 --> 14:10.560
So the empire functionally believes that anything not on those roads does not exist.

14:10.640 --> 14:12.120
It's an epistemological blind spot.

14:12.120 --> 14:17.200
Gaius realizes that the Roman infrastructure is just a superficial layer resting on top

14:17.200 --> 14:19.840
of an older, infinitely more complex geometry.

14:20.440 --> 14:22.960
The fugitive is using the logic of water.

14:23.500 --> 14:29.040
He's flowing into the paths of least resistance, constantly seeking the highest volume of future

14:29.040 --> 14:29.740
admissibility.

14:30.240 --> 14:33.640
Which completely reframes the power dynamic of the whole pursuit.

14:33.640 --> 14:37.440
The Romans think they are hunting a terrified, helpless animal.

14:38.280 --> 14:44.560
But Gaius realizes he is engaging in a high stakes topological war against a master navigator.

14:44.920 --> 14:48.280
The Romans are playing a rigid game of checkers on a two dimensional board.

14:48.400 --> 14:48.640
Right.

14:48.720 --> 14:52.220
While the fugitive is flowing through a three dimensional fluid dynamics simulation.

14:52.520 --> 14:54.460
He was not escaping the parable observes.

14:54.940 --> 14:55.880
He was navigating.

14:55.880 --> 14:56.920
I love that line.

14:57.160 --> 15:02.520
But this realization forces Gaius to confront a severe problem regarding physical identification

15:02.520 --> 15:03.680
over time, doesn't it?

15:03.940 --> 15:09.000
It does because he is now tracking a trajectory through the water network, not a static visual

15:09.000 --> 15:09.560
profile.

15:09.660 --> 15:09.800
Right.

15:09.980 --> 15:10.940
The old data is useless.

15:11.460 --> 15:15.320
He follows these hidden paths deep into the desert for weeks.

15:15.920 --> 15:21.380
And eventually he arrives at a remote village and finds three entirely different men who could

15:21.380 --> 15:22.900
logically be the fugitive.

15:22.900 --> 15:26.300
Because the original man left his cloak behind on day one.

15:27.040 --> 15:31.080
By week three, his physical state, his substrate has completely transformed.

15:31.740 --> 15:33.200
He's probably traded garments.

15:33.520 --> 15:35.000
His skin is weathered by the sun.

15:35.260 --> 15:36.100
He's grown a beard.

15:36.500 --> 15:40.040
The visual snapshot the Romans recorded is just a ghost now.

15:40.920 --> 15:46.180
This brings us to the core philosophical puzzle Fliction introduces as the snake principle.

15:46.340 --> 15:46.960
The snake principle.

15:47.120 --> 15:47.360
Yes.

15:47.360 --> 15:52.560
If the physical appearance of the man has completely changed and the original material he wore is

15:52.560 --> 15:56.560
gone, on what basis can we claim he is still the same continuous identity?

15:57.260 --> 15:59.600
The parable lays this out with such a beautiful simplicity.

15:59.780 --> 16:01.760
It says, identity is not what remains.

16:02.100 --> 16:03.280
Identity is what continues.

16:03.600 --> 16:06.760
It gives some great examples from the natural world and historical texts.

16:06.900 --> 16:07.000
Yeah.

16:07.040 --> 16:09.900
Like a lizard detaches his tail to escape the jaws of a predator.

16:10.360 --> 16:13.380
The tail remains behind, thrashing in the dirt.

16:13.620 --> 16:14.820
But the lizard continues.

16:14.820 --> 16:19.400
Or the mantle of leadership, falling from an aging prophet to a young student.

16:20.180 --> 16:23.840
The original man perishes, but the calling continues in the next.

16:24.420 --> 16:30.080
The underlying systems theory here separates the physical matter, the substrate, from the

16:30.080 --> 16:32.080
organization of that matter, the pattern.

16:32.280 --> 16:37.100
The text provides a formalization showing that while the substrate inevitably degrades and

16:37.100 --> 16:40.080
changes over time, the pattern can be preserved.

16:40.080 --> 16:45.040
And to illustrate this, the source uses a variation of the classic ship of Theses paradox.

16:45.200 --> 16:46.340
A really brilliant variation.

16:46.700 --> 16:49.380
It shows a visual progression of a ship across four stages.

16:49.560 --> 16:51.880
Stage one, the original ship sets sail.

16:52.000 --> 16:55.500
Stage two, a storm breaks the oars and they are replaced with new wood.

16:55.580 --> 16:56.440
Stage three.

16:57.180 --> 17:00.960
Years later, the hull rots and is entirely rebuilt and new sails are woven.

17:01.440 --> 17:01.900
Stage four.

17:02.480 --> 17:06.180
Eventually, every single plank, nail, and rope has been swapped out.

17:06.720 --> 17:09.300
Literally not one atom of the original ship remains.

17:09.300 --> 17:12.880
And Gaius wrestles with this while talking to an old shepherd in the desert.

17:13.100 --> 17:17.260
He asks, how can the same thing remain the same if nothing remains the same?

17:17.300 --> 17:21.520
It is the ultimate anxiety of an empire that relies on material evidence.

17:22.040 --> 17:22.520
Exactly.

17:22.860 --> 17:28.220
If I cannot physically point to the original wood, how do I have jurisdiction over the ship?

17:28.780 --> 17:32.720
Now, I want to push back on the traditional interpretation of this paradox for a second.

17:32.720 --> 17:39.260
Because often, people use the ship of Theseus to argue that identity is just an illusion.

17:39.640 --> 17:39.880
Right.

17:39.960 --> 17:41.560
They say it's just a naming convention.

17:41.880 --> 17:42.000
Yeah.

17:42.040 --> 17:45.220
They say, well, if all the parts are replaced, it's obviously a different ship and we just

17:45.220 --> 17:46.440
use the same name for convenience.

17:46.720 --> 17:51.120
But what if the new hull is made of a lighter wood and the new sails catch the wind better?

17:51.400 --> 17:52.840
The ship is actually improving.

17:52.840 --> 17:56.180
The ship is fundamentally faster and more capable now.

17:56.180 --> 17:59.000
Well, hasn't the pattern actually changed, not just the substrate?

17:59.940 --> 18:04.180
That is exactly where Fliction's interpretation elevates the concept.

18:04.720 --> 18:08.400
The parable doesn't view the replacement of parts as a threat to identity.

18:08.920 --> 18:12.660
It views replacement as the very mechanism of identity.

18:12.860 --> 18:13.360
Oh, wow.

18:13.580 --> 18:17.100
The shepherd answers Gaius by saying, it is the same if the snake keeps going.

18:17.100 --> 18:19.140
It isn't if you only look at the skin.

18:19.860 --> 18:22.940
Fliction defines identity as the continuity of reachability.

18:23.140 --> 18:24.280
Continuity of reachability.

18:24.280 --> 18:30.960
Yes. The text explicitly states, repair equals the preservation of future access.

18:31.320 --> 18:35.680
So you don't replace the rotting hull of a ship because you want to preserve the historical

18:35.680 --> 18:37.300
integrity of the specific timber.

18:37.460 --> 18:39.660
No, you replace the hull so the ship does not sink.

18:39.780 --> 18:39.980
Right.

18:40.120 --> 18:44.420
You swap out the substrate specifically to ensure that the pattern retains its ability to

18:44.420 --> 18:45.320
navigate the ocean.

18:45.640 --> 18:49.160
So the goal isn't historical preservation, it's functional survival.

18:49.380 --> 18:54.060
Exactly. If the newly repaired ship, even with lighter wood and better sails,

18:54.280 --> 18:59.000
can still navigate to the same ports, can still carry cargo and still fulfills its intended

18:59.000 --> 19:02.980
trajectory, it maintains its identity within the admissibility field.

19:03.300 --> 19:07.120
Identity follows future accessibility, not material permanence.

19:07.200 --> 19:07.480
Yes.

19:07.480 --> 19:09.760
This completely flips the Roman perspective.

19:10.560 --> 19:15.700
The observer on the shore sees a loss of original material when the broken oar is tossed into the sea.

19:16.400 --> 19:21.480
But the system itself, the ship, the crew, the voyage, experiences survival and growth.

19:21.840 --> 19:23.200
Losing a part isn't a failure.

19:23.600 --> 19:24.300
It's an adaptation.

19:24.960 --> 19:27.420
Think about the biological reality of your own body.

19:27.420 --> 19:29.220
You know, listening to this right now.

19:29.960 --> 19:35.720
Over a span of roughly seven to ten years, virtually every cellular structure in your body

19:35.720 --> 19:38.240
undergoes a process of death and replacement.

19:38.480 --> 19:39.860
Your entire substrate turns over.

19:40.000 --> 19:40.520
Entirely.

19:41.200 --> 19:45.480
The physical matter sitting in your chair right now is not the same physical matter

19:45.480 --> 19:47.340
that was sitting in the classroom a decade ago.

19:47.620 --> 19:52.140
Yet, your memories, your relational ties, your core values,

19:52.140 --> 19:55.580
the complex pattern of you persists.

19:55.940 --> 20:01.160
You are an ongoing process of cellular repair designed to preserve your future access to life.

20:01.380 --> 20:03.280
It's mind-blowing when you frame it like that.

20:03.540 --> 20:07.480
And Gaius is forced to realize that if an individual can swap out their physical substrate

20:07.480 --> 20:12.200
and maintain their identity, a movement can do the exact same thing on a macroscopic scale.

20:12.320 --> 20:14.320
Oh, right. Because he's tracking a movement now.

20:14.480 --> 20:14.760
Yes.

20:15.140 --> 20:18.100
He follows the trajectory of the fugitive's impact for years.

20:18.100 --> 20:24.560
This pursuit eventually leads him out of the desert and into the bustling metropolis of Antioch in the AD 30s.

20:25.020 --> 20:30.260
And the organizational structure he encounters there just completely short-circuits his Roman understanding of power.

20:30.460 --> 20:32.480
Because he's no longer looking for one man.

20:32.920 --> 20:33.700
The parable notes,

20:34.240 --> 20:35.620
He had been hunting one man.

20:35.920 --> 20:38.200
The idea had already become 200 people.

20:38.360 --> 20:40.040
The fugitive's trajectory had multiplied.

20:40.440 --> 20:40.600
Yeah.

20:40.740 --> 20:43.440
But it did so in a way that defied traditional hierarchy.

20:43.440 --> 20:49.560
The text observes no single hand, no single voice, no center, and yet it kept moving.

20:50.060 --> 20:56.220
This is where we get into the concept of the community manifold, or what the source visually titles the City of White Cloaks.

20:56.640 --> 20:57.920
The City of White Cloaks.

20:58.000 --> 21:02.260
The Roman Empire is entirely predicated on a strict, centralized hierarchy.

21:02.720 --> 21:03.820
A legion has a general.

21:04.180 --> 21:05.300
A province has a governor.

21:05.760 --> 21:09.220
The power flows from a single point at the top down to the base.

21:09.220 --> 21:13.440
Even when they face rebellions, those rebellions usually mirror the Roman structure.

21:13.680 --> 21:16.000
They have a charismatic king or a central warlord.

21:16.260 --> 21:18.880
And the Roman strategy for dealing with that is simple and brutal.

21:19.620 --> 21:20.140
Decapitation.

21:20.480 --> 21:20.620
Right.

21:20.720 --> 21:26.360
You find the central node, you eliminate it, and the entire network shatters into isolated, powerless fragments.

21:26.620 --> 21:32.020
But Gaius analyzes the community in Antioch and realizes there is no central node to target.

21:32.020 --> 21:40.860
The movement had evolved from a fragile, one-teacher, many-listeners model into a deeply resilient, everyone-teaching, everyone-learning topology.

21:41.060 --> 21:43.940
The visual diagrams in the text map this out so clearly.

21:44.460 --> 21:48.160
A centralized star graph is labeled fragile, easy to sever.

21:48.400 --> 21:56.300
But the community structure in Antioch is drawn as this dense, overlapping web where every single point connects to multiple other points.

21:56.420 --> 22:01.460
And it is labeled dense mutuality, uncuttable, alive.

22:01.460 --> 22:05.420
I have to pause here and challenge the idealism of that word, uncuttable.

22:05.580 --> 22:05.980
Fair enough.

22:06.040 --> 22:13.360
Because we've seen plenty of decentralized, leaderless movements in modern history, social uprisings, internet collectives, autonomous zones.

22:13.840 --> 22:17.260
And while they were initially very hard to police, they often collapse from the inside.

22:17.480 --> 22:17.780
They do.

22:17.860 --> 22:23.600
They fracture defactions, they lose their cohesive purpose, or they simply dissolve from a lack of coordinated logistics.

22:24.300 --> 22:26.980
Is fliction romanticizing the concept of a manifold here?

22:27.360 --> 22:30.220
Or is there a mathematical reason this specific community survived?

22:30.220 --> 22:32.360
It's a really crucial distinction to make.

22:32.920 --> 22:35.620
Not every leaderless group is a functional manifold.

22:35.780 --> 22:36.720
Okay, so what's the difference?

22:37.440 --> 22:43.520
Well, a mob is decentralized, but it is highly fragile because it is built entirely on temporary emotional momentum.

22:44.400 --> 22:51.200
The text specifies that a true manifold, denoted mathematically as M equals X, R.

22:51.540 --> 22:53.260
Wait, what do X and R stand for?

22:53.800 --> 22:56.720
X is the individuals, and R is the relationships.

22:56.720 --> 23:01.840
A true manifold requires dense, sustained relationships between the individuals.

23:02.380 --> 23:05.200
It lists the specific threads that weave this structure together.

23:05.940 --> 23:07.240
Shared food and hospitality.

23:07.980 --> 23:09.240
Mutual economic provision.

23:09.840 --> 23:10.660
Caring for the sick.

23:11.300 --> 23:12.080
Shared counsel.

23:12.180 --> 23:14.480
So the connections aren't just ideological agreement.

23:14.580 --> 23:16.240
They are vital survival logistics.

23:16.240 --> 23:16.880
Exactly.

23:17.320 --> 23:21.460
The mathematics of a manifold dictate a property of local change, global continuity.

23:22.000 --> 23:29.380
If you remove one individual from this dense web of mutual aid, or if a local leader shifts position, the local coordinate changes.

23:29.500 --> 23:30.600
But the whole thing doesn't fall apart.

23:30.800 --> 23:31.040
Right.

23:31.240 --> 23:39.100
The global continuity, the overall structure of the manifold, persists without interruption because the load is instantly distributed across a dozen other connections.

23:39.580 --> 23:42.100
It's like a mycelial network in an old growth forest.

23:42.100 --> 23:43.520
Yes, perfect analogy.

23:43.920 --> 23:49.680
If a single mushroom is plucked from the surface, the subterranean root system barely registers the loss.

23:50.460 --> 23:51.980
The nutrients simply reroute.

23:53.080 --> 23:57.900
Gaius realizes that arresting one person in this community would be entirely futile.

23:58.160 --> 23:58.860
The text says,

23:59.360 --> 24:00.620
The coordinator had disappeared.

24:01.200 --> 24:03.040
The structure required no center.

24:03.600 --> 24:04.660
It had become a pattern.

24:05.120 --> 24:06.780
And the pattern kept reproducing.

24:07.100 --> 24:11.200
Gaius observes the community sharing a meal, teaching children and pooling resources.

24:11.200 --> 24:13.960
And he summarizes the Roman nightmare perfectly.

24:14.700 --> 24:15.000
He says,

24:15.220 --> 24:18.080
There is no central fire, yet all are warmed.

24:18.720 --> 24:23.540
They were looking for a king to crucify, and they found an interconnected network of mutual care.

24:23.660 --> 24:25.280
You cannot drive a nail through a topology.

24:25.380 --> 24:26.320
You really can't.

24:26.500 --> 24:29.200
The identity of the fugitive didn't just survive, it migrated.

24:29.700 --> 24:33.620
It moved from a single physical point to a distributed behavioral pattern.

24:33.620 --> 24:37.240
The original teacher's physical presence was no longer the critical dependency,

24:37.740 --> 24:43.880
because his trajectory, his method of reachability, had been downloaded into the daily actions of the entire community.

24:44.180 --> 24:46.780
It is the ultimate manifestation of the snake principle.

24:47.520 --> 24:50.680
The physical body of the founder was simply the initial substrate.

24:51.340 --> 24:55.760
The movement itself, the dense mutuality, is the enduring pattern.

24:55.760 --> 24:59.680
Think about the organizations or communities you interact with every day.

25:00.180 --> 25:01.600
Are they fragile hierarchies?

25:02.180 --> 25:07.840
Do they rely entirely on the brilliance of one founder, or the approval of one micromanaging boss?

25:08.180 --> 25:12.720
If so, that structure is incredibly vulnerable to a single point of failure.

25:13.060 --> 25:19.380
The parable argues that to achieve true resilience in a hostile environment, a system must evolve into a manifold.

25:20.080 --> 25:23.460
It must distribute its vital functions across a web of mutual support.

25:23.460 --> 25:27.620
And when an empire encounters a resilient manifold that it cannot control or sever,

25:28.020 --> 25:30.380
it inevitably reacts with the pathology of control.

25:30.740 --> 25:33.180
We see this vividly in the next phase of the parable.

25:33.240 --> 25:34.860
Right, because the Romans don't just give up.

25:34.980 --> 25:37.940
No. Frustrated by a network that constantly evades capture,

25:38.200 --> 25:41.440
the Romans retreat into what they believe is their ultimate stronghold,

25:41.760 --> 25:43.760
the absolute cataloging of the past.

25:44.100 --> 25:48.840
This brings us to a sequence that is visually stunning and conceptually terrifying.

25:49.100 --> 25:53.040
An older Gaius takes a young student to visit a subterranean complex

25:53.040 --> 25:55.820
referred to as the Infinite Archive.

25:55.820 --> 26:00.300
The artwork here transitions into these sweeping, cavernous perspectives.

26:00.680 --> 26:05.620
Imagine an endless, brutalist metropolis of shelves and vaults extending into the darkness.

26:06.000 --> 26:10.180
Watched over by massive, imposing statues of Roman emperors.

26:10.320 --> 26:13.260
It is an architectural manifestation of state obsession.

26:14.100 --> 26:18.200
The archive contains every artifact the empire has ever seized or cataloged.

26:18.200 --> 26:26.140
Cloaks, animal skins, ancient wax tablets, tax decrees, architectural measurements, legal arguments, genealogical records.

26:26.280 --> 26:26.720
Everything.

26:27.100 --> 26:32.220
The parable describes it not as a geographic location, but as a warehouse of everything that had ever been kept.

26:32.360 --> 26:32.580
Quote,

26:32.840 --> 26:36.180
Everything is tagged, everything is placed, nothing is forgotten.

26:36.940 --> 26:40.140
The Romans have constructed the ultimate repository of fixed coordinates.

26:40.400 --> 26:42.680
They have frozen millions of moments in time.

26:42.680 --> 26:47.340
They place them in meticulously organized rows and convince themselves that by hoarding the past,

26:47.440 --> 26:49.740
they are guaranteeing the immortality of their civilization.

26:50.140 --> 26:55.640
But the young student, walking alongside Gaius, looks out over the suffocating canyon of dead objects,

26:56.120 --> 26:59.440
and he articulates the fatal flaw in the Roman psychology.

26:59.740 --> 27:00.320
What does he say?

27:00.660 --> 27:01.140
He says,

27:01.140 --> 27:03.200
They didn't build this to understand.

27:03.380 --> 27:04.640
They built it to remember.

27:05.380 --> 27:09.300
Enfliction drops this capitalized, definitive statement onto the page.

27:09.800 --> 27:11.560
The warehouse contains every cloak.

27:11.840 --> 27:13.760
The warehouse contains no fugitive.

27:14.060 --> 27:14.380
Wow.

27:15.300 --> 27:16.800
It's a chilling realization.

27:17.400 --> 27:20.740
They possess every piece of physical evidence the man ever left behind.

27:21.300 --> 27:22.560
They have the shed skin.

27:22.760 --> 27:23.720
But the snake is gone.

27:23.960 --> 27:28.220
The warehouse is nothing more than a meticulously organized graveyard of dead states.

27:28.220 --> 27:32.280
This introduces a critical distinction between storage and true preservation.

27:33.020 --> 27:34.420
The Romans conflate the two.

27:35.000 --> 27:39.440
They believe that hoarding the physical substrate of an event is the same as preserving its meaning.

27:39.620 --> 27:41.280
But storage is a passive dead action.

27:41.400 --> 27:41.840
Exactly.

27:42.240 --> 27:46.020
You can store a million data points and still possess zero functional capability.

27:46.640 --> 27:49.040
True preservation requires maintaining reachability.

27:49.320 --> 27:52.740
This reminds me of the debate between building a traditional natural history museum

27:52.740 --> 27:55.800
versus funding a living wildlife preserve.

27:55.980 --> 27:57.380
Oh, that's a brilliant comparison.

27:57.380 --> 28:00.680
Because a museum operates on the logic of the Infinite Archive.

28:00.860 --> 28:06.640
It takes an animal, kills it, stuffs it, and mounts it perfectly under glass with a precise taxonomy tag.

28:06.820 --> 28:07.960
It is perfectly stored.

28:08.140 --> 28:08.340
Yes.

28:08.660 --> 28:10.720
But it is completely removed from the ecosystem.

28:11.340 --> 28:13.580
It can no longer hunt, mate, or evolve.

28:14.000 --> 28:15.680
It has zero admissible futures.

28:16.100 --> 28:17.400
Whereas a wildlife preserve.

28:17.760 --> 28:21.820
A wildlife preserve is messy, uncontrolled, and difficult to categorize.

28:22.020 --> 28:24.720
But it preserves the living trajectory of the species.

28:24.720 --> 28:26.320
The museum preserves the state.

28:26.720 --> 28:28.380
The preserve protects the possibilities.

28:29.020 --> 28:36.280
Fliction includes a diagram titled CLIO, the projected archive, which mathematically models how an archive functions as a projection.

28:36.480 --> 28:36.660
Right.

28:36.780 --> 28:40.120
Flattening a complex multidimensional reality into a static summary.

28:40.120 --> 28:41.120
Yes.

28:41.120 --> 28:50.260
The equation, S sub pi equals the log volume of the inverse projection of hem, suggests that every archived object hides a vast volume of unrecorded possibilities.

28:50.580 --> 28:54.000
The text argues that a true archive should not be a vault of dead artifacts.

28:54.520 --> 28:57.560
It should be a machine for generating future trajectories.

28:57.920 --> 29:02.380
An archive only possesses living value if it's constantly being reinterpreted.

29:02.380 --> 29:06.200
If new connections are actively forging new paths forward.

29:06.620 --> 29:11.960
The tragedy of modern society is that we are actively building our own infinite archives right now.

29:12.480 --> 29:14.180
We hoard exabytes of data.

29:14.340 --> 29:19.520
We record every interaction, save every email, photograph every meal, back up every file.

29:19.780 --> 29:22.320
We're obsessed with perfectly storing the state of our lives.

29:22.320 --> 29:28.140
But in our desperation to catalog the past, we often paralyze our ability to navigate the future.

29:28.620 --> 29:34.520
We have an infinite repository of data, but we're losing the living relationship with our own trajectory.

29:34.800 --> 29:37.720
The Romans believed they were storing evidence, the text notes.

29:38.180 --> 29:40.540
What they were really storing was possibility.

29:40.980 --> 29:44.200
But by freezing it in the archive, they neutralized its potential.

29:44.460 --> 29:46.780
They turned living energy into an inert artifact.

29:47.120 --> 29:50.500
The future is entirely indifferent to how well you have cataloged your past.

29:50.860 --> 29:51.900
The future moves on.

29:51.900 --> 29:59.320
Which brings us to the grand synthesis of Gaius' journey and the final profound philosophical weight of the source material.

30:00.400 --> 30:08.220
If reality isn't a collection of stored objects, if it isn't a mountain of evidence in a warehouse, what is the actual substance of the world?

30:08.580 --> 30:12.900
The parable answers this in its concluding section through the concept of the admissibility field.

30:13.180 --> 30:14.120
The admissibility field.

30:14.500 --> 30:19.440
This is where the mathematical metaphors reach their absolute peak, fundamentally redefining ontology.

30:19.440 --> 30:23.300
Lissian asserts, reality is not a collection of things.

30:23.920 --> 30:25.580
Reality is what remains reachable.

30:26.100 --> 30:34.500
The physical objects we interact with, the visible roads we drive on, these are merely the surface traces of a much deeper underlying current of possibility.

30:34.740 --> 30:39.640
The text grounds this seemingly mystical idea in a very specific, elegant formula.

30:40.080 --> 30:43.080
Phi of X equals the log volume of A of X.

30:43.080 --> 30:48.160
Let's break down the mechanics of this equation because it serves as the conceptual key to the entire narrative.

30:48.360 --> 30:51.340
Okay, so phi of X represents the potential of future accessibility.

30:51.800 --> 30:54.260
A of X is the actual volume of admissible futures.

30:54.500 --> 30:58.960
The total number of viable paths that remain open to a system from its current position.

30:58.960 --> 31:05.380
And the logarithm simply scales that massive, multidimensional volume into a comprehensible metric.

31:05.640 --> 31:06.200
Exactly.

31:06.420 --> 31:09.500
So it's calculating the sheer amount of options you have left.

31:09.760 --> 31:12.320
And the parable pairs this with a differential equation, right?

31:12.520 --> 31:15.200
dx over dt equals the gradient of phi.

31:15.300 --> 31:15.580
Yes.

31:15.940 --> 31:22.300
The gradient represents the direction of steepest descent, the direction that leads toward the greatest increase in future possibilities.

31:22.300 --> 31:31.320
The equation suggests that a surviving system over time will naturally and inevitably move in the direction that maximizes its future options.

31:31.400 --> 31:34.880
Now I want to translate this out of the abstract math and into human behavior.

31:35.360 --> 31:37.920
How does a person actually move up that gradient?

31:38.140 --> 31:41.620
Are we talking about a subconscious psychological drive toward optionality?

31:41.860 --> 31:44.640
It manifests as the instinct for adaptation and resilience.

31:45.380 --> 31:46.920
Think back to the ship of Theseus.

31:47.260 --> 31:49.580
Why does the crew replace the rotting wood?

31:50.020 --> 31:52.040
They aren't doing calculus on the deck.

31:52.300 --> 31:54.780
They are reacting to the gradient of admissibility.

31:55.120 --> 31:58.820
If they keep the old wood, the ship sinks and their future volume drops to zero.

31:59.320 --> 32:04.380
By swapping the substrate, they move uphill along the gradient, restoring their reachability.

32:05.160 --> 32:11.620
Human beings do this intuitively when we abandon a failing strategy, leave a toxic environment, or learn a new skill.

32:11.820 --> 32:15.540
We are constantly navigating toward the higher ground of open possibility.

32:15.800 --> 32:18.200
The fugitive wasn't just surviving by running away.

32:18.200 --> 32:22.480
He was flowing, like the water networks he used, toward greater possibility.

32:22.900 --> 32:25.960
He was a master of navigating the admissibility field.

32:26.060 --> 32:31.940
He kept his options open while the Romans were constantly trying to drag him down into the valley of a locked prison cell,

32:32.320 --> 32:35.600
a state where his future volume would violently collapse to zero.

32:35.600 --> 32:38.140
The Romans function as agents of closure.

32:38.420 --> 32:44.860
They exist to collapse the wave function of human potential into a single, dead, manageable state.

32:45.240 --> 32:50.000
The fugitive and the manifold community that followed him function as agents of admissibility.

32:50.400 --> 32:52.180
They exist to keep the future open.

32:52.180 --> 32:56.800
Infliction's ultimate revelation is that this dynamic isn't just a political struggle.

32:57.140 --> 32:58.860
It is the fundamental structure of reality.

32:59.260 --> 33:01.540
Reality equals what remains reachable.

33:01.920 --> 33:06.000
The parable maps Gaius' entire life arc across this realization.

33:06.620 --> 33:08.020
It's a three-stage evolution.

33:08.720 --> 33:10.260
Stage one, find the man.

33:10.600 --> 33:12.200
That was his rigid Roman phase.

33:12.280 --> 33:12.480
Yeah.

33:12.680 --> 33:15.880
Obsessed with capturing a physical body and securing a state.

33:16.040 --> 33:17.800
Stage two, find the path.

33:17.800 --> 33:22.580
That occurred on the cliffside, discovering the hidden water networks and understanding reachability.

33:22.940 --> 33:25.560
And finally, stage three, find what survives.

33:26.060 --> 33:31.120
That is the moment he stands in the infinite archive and realizes he was never hunting a man at all.

33:31.500 --> 33:34.800
He had spent his entire life studying the geometry of survival.

33:34.960 --> 33:37.540
It is a profound, hard-won transformation.

33:38.160 --> 33:42.700
He begins as a loyal enforcer of the state, demanding rigid evidence and static records.

33:42.700 --> 33:50.380
He ends his life as a philosopher of continuous trajectories, deeply aware that the essence of life can never be contained in a warehouse.

33:50.860 --> 33:53.460
The visual conclusion of the graphic novel is incredibly moving.

33:53.940 --> 33:59.340
The older Gaius and his young student have finally left the suffocating, claustrophobic air of the infinite archive.

33:59.420 --> 34:02.300
They climb up to the surface and sit on a crumbling stone wall.

34:02.640 --> 34:06.180
Looking out over a massive, glowing river delta as the sun sets,

34:06.620 --> 34:11.660
the water is branching out into countless glittering pathways, weaving endlessly into the horizon.

34:11.660 --> 34:14.980
And Gaius voices the profound truth he has learned.

34:15.460 --> 34:17.280
The roads didn't lead back to the city.

34:17.620 --> 34:18.620
They led everywhere.

